        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-25T10:11:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It sounds like we\u0027re not sure whether this code is needed? It seems incorrect to clear the upstream rules when something happens to the downstream. I don\u0027t think we should keep this code \"just in case\". We should design the code so that we can guarantee that all the cleanup paths will run at the right time.\n\nMaybe remove this code and add tests to see if you can get into a situation where the rules are incorrectly not cleared?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 964,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 964,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "8e992b1fa809045bb49df90b523e841036b4aac4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bfd27e44_e7b2c130",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/android/net/ip/IpServer.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 964,
      "author": {
        "id": 1961665
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-29T14:51:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Current code is just follows the old logic. But your concerns in #969 and #777 make sense, I just did a test and found that when the upstream is removed (by remove the SIM card), the neighbor entries in linux kernel won\u0027t be removed. Which means, when the same upstream come back again, the existing neighbor entries won\u0027t be added back to the bpf map. Maybe preserve the downstream rules and modify the upstreamIfindex to 0 when the upstream interface is gone is a better solution.",
      "parentUuid": "bffa66de_29fe958a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 964,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 964,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "8e992b1fa809045bb49df90b523e841036b4aac4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bc7cecb4_c4695d76",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/android/net/ip/IpServer.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 969,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-25T10:11:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wonder... if we don\u0027t have an upstream, is returning early the right thing?\n\nClearly, we  cannot forward traffic if we don\u0027t have an upstream. But if we don\u0027t call BpfCoordinator#addIpv6DownstreamRule, then we will just forget about this neighbor. If later on the the upstream changes, we won\u0027t start downstream forwarding to this neighbour. Downstream forwarding to this neighbour will start only when we get another NeighborEvent for it. So you could argue that we should instead add a rule with an upstream index of zero - it won\u0027t actually forward traffic, but  at least it will be in mIpv6DownstreamRules already, and when the upstream changes, then we will call onNewInterface on it.\n\nOn the other hand, you could argue that returning early here is correct, because when later on the upstream changes, it will probably have an IPv6 prefix that is not the same prefix as this neighbour, so there\u0027s no point forwarding to this neighbour anyway.\n\nMaybe we can return early here, but add a comment that says:\n\n    // Ignore NeighborEvents if there is no upstream. If the upstream later changes, it\n    // will likely have a different IPv6 prefix from this NeighborEvent anyway. So there\n    // is no point adding this neighbor to the IPv6 rules.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 969,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 969,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "8e992b1fa809045bb49df90b523e841036b4aac4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
